THE DISCRIMINATOR CHANGED, and the old one is kept here because it was
right about the facts and wrong about what to do with them.  It read: "a
file is deleted when it has NO device text, and guarded when it has some
... those stay guarded, because cutting a fragment out of a file this tree
must keep byte-recoverable buys nothing NVRTC can see: it already sees
nothing there."  Every clause of that is true.  It answers the wrong
question.  The rule is not "can NVRTC see it" -- a guard already settles
that -- it is "is `csrc/` device text and nothing else", and a guarded host
launcher is still host C++ sitting in `csrc/`.  So the discriminator is now:

    host C++ is REMOVED, wherever it is, whatever hides it.

Three things fell out of running that over the whole set instead of over
the three fragments named above, and all three were surprises:

  * The three named fragments were the SMALL case.  `pos_enc.cuh` alone hid
    591 lines -- fourteen `cudaError_t` launchers -- behind one guard.
  * Not all of it was guarded.  Thirteen host launchers in `cascade.cuh`,
    `decode.cuh`, `mla.cuh` and `prefill.cuh`, 1,055 lines, sat OUTSIDE any
    guard and survived only by being uninstantiated templates.  That is a
    much weaker shield than a guard: it holds while nothing names them and
    while the frontend defers lookup of non-dependent names in a template
    body.  A guard is a decision; non-instantiation is a coincidence.
  * `QKVLayoutToString` and `PosEncodingModeToString` had ZERO callers, not
    one apiece.  Both were briefed as having one.  The single hit each was
    the sentence in this file describing them.

