These six sat here for a provenance reason -- they exist BECAUSE FlashInfer
asks for them, so they were filed under FlashInfer.  That is the wrong axis.
What they DO is impersonate a header this compiler does not ship, which is
also what `cuda_fp16.h`, `cuda_bf16.h`, `cuda_fp8.h`, `cuda_fp4.h`,
`cooperative_groups.h` and `cuda/{cmath,pipeline,std/limits}` do, and those
eight sat under `csrc/src/` for the mirror-image reason: our own text asks for
them too.  All fourteen are now in `csrc/shim/`, filed by role.

The move cannot be seen from inside a compile.  NVRTC has no `-I`: it matches
the literal string in the directive against the names in `includeNames[]`, and
those names are unchanged, because for a shim THE NAME IS THE CONTRACT -- a
file called `cuda_fp16.h` is answering for NVIDIA's `cuda_fp16.h` and cannot
be renamed without ceasing to do its job.  nvcc needed one line: a second
`-iquote` at each of the four sites that pass the first.

One row in this table changed, and not because of the move: see the
correction near the top.  Four rows LEFT it, and that was not the move
either: see HOST C++ above.  Every file still listed moved, together, from
`csrc/vendor/flashinfer/` to `csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/`, byte for byte.

WHAT THE TRANSFORM RECOVERS IS NOW SMALLER, and saying so is the point of
keeping this document.  Twelve files still come back by steps 1 and 2 alone
-- the ones with a `0` in the `guards` column, which is what a `0` there now
means.  Twelve need step 3, and step 3 needs the upstream tarball, because
what a `// PIE: REMOVED` marker records is what a file no longer contains.
The property this document asserts is therefore weaker than it was, and it is
still checkable: every line that REMAINS in the twenty-four files below is
upstream's, and every line that does not is named, counted and attributed.
The columns are re-derived from the tree by the same code the test runs, not
adjusted by hand -- which is the failure this file exists to have caught once
already, at `attention/mla.cuh`.
The shim group was done first for a reason that turned out to generalise: it
was the one role whose files are neither upstream's nor included by relative
path from an upstream file, so it moved without editing a single upstream
byte.  Moving the closure as one subtree bought the same property for a
harder case, and the 58 relative directives are why.
